# `actuarial::Operator<Signature>`

A named callable with a fixed signature: the common form of every reusable transformation in the actuarial surface.

## Signature

```cpp
bool actuarial::Operator<Signature>::defined() const noexcept;
std::string_view actuarial::Operator<Signature>::key() const noexcept;
explicit actuarial::Operator<Signature>::operator bool() const noexcept;
Result actuarial::Operator<Signature>::operator()(Args... args) const;
```

## What It Represents

An `Operator<R(A...)>` holds a callable taking `A...` and returning `R`, together with a descriptive **key**. `Signature` fixes the types at compile time, so an operator that values a cashflow cannot be handed a triangle. The key names the transformation for logs and diagnostics. Operators are cheap to copy and are values: the library returns them from [`operators::value`](../valuation/Basis.md), `deductible`, `chain_ladder` and the other builders, and [`make_operator`](make_operator.md) creates your own with the same contract, so a custom rule can be used anywhere a library one can. A default-constructed operator is empty.

## Methods

| Method | What it does |
|---|---|
| [`defined`](Operator/defined.md) | Tells whether a callable is installed. |
| [`key`](Operator/key.md) | The descriptive name given at construction. |
| [`operator bool`](Operator/operator_bool.md) | The explicit boolean form of `defined`. |
| [`operator()`](Operator/operator_call.md) | Calls the stored callable. |

## Practical Examples

### Example 1: a scalar operator

`make_operator` wraps a lambda; the operator reports its key and is called like the lambda.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::Operator<double(double)> square = actuarial::make_operator<double(double)>("square", [](double x) { return x * x; });
  out.writeln("key:", square.key(), " defined:", square.defined());
  out.writeln("square(3) =", square(3.0));
}
```

### Example 2: an operator on a Series

The same contract carries whole vectors, so one call does the work of a loop.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto cube = actuarial::make_operator<pluss::Series(const pluss::Series &)>("cube", [](const pluss::Series &x) { return x.mul(x).mul(x); });
  out.writeln(cube(pluss::make_series({1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0})).items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3: the library builds operators too

The operators the library returns are the same type, with keys that name them.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const actuarial::Mortality mortality = actuarial::mortality::from_qx(pluss::make_series({0.01, 0.02, 0.03}));
  const actuarial::Valuation value = actuarial::operators::value({mortality, actuarial::discount::flat(0.04)});
  out.writeln("key:", value.key(), " 3-year annuity =", value(actuarial::cashflow::annuity(3)).item<double>());
}
```

### Example 4: an empty operator

A default-constructed operator is not defined, has an empty key, and throws when called.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

namespace actuarial = pluss::ma::actuarial;
namespace life = actuarial::life_contingencies;
namespace losses = actuarial::losses;
namespace models = actuarial::models;
namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
using namespace pluss::tils;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  const auto report = [&](const auto &call) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception &e) {
      std::string text(e.what());
      if (const auto cut = text.find("Triggered"); cut != std::string::npos) text.erase(cut);
      while (!text.empty() && text.back() == '\n') text.pop_back();
      out.writeln(text);
    }
  };
  const actuarial::Operator<double(double)> none;
  out.writeln("defined:", none.defined(), " key empty:", none.key().empty());
  report([&] { (void)none(1.0); });
}
```

## Changelog

- **0.1**: initial operator-first actuarial surface.

## See Also

[`make_operator`](make_operator.md) builds one, and [`Valuation`](../valuation/Basis.md) is the library operator you will meet first.

